  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nModel guidance is now trending toward a noticeably less active\nmonsoon period during the latter half of the week, largely in part\ndue to the subtropical high strengthening and setting up over\nwest Texas through our region. The main forecast concern will\nquickly shift from monsoon storms to extreme heat by Thursday.\nEnsembles show the high shifting southward starting Wednesday and\nthen expanding into our region Thursday into Friday as H5 heights\nover our region quickly rise from 591-593dm Wednesday to \n596-598dm by Friday. Southerly dry flow will pick up across our\narea on Wednesday, decreasing rain chances further before chances\nlikely drop below 10-15% starting Thursday as strong subsidence\nspreads over the region. \n\nTemperatures should quickly respond to the increasing heights and\nthe decreasing monsoon activity later this week. NBM forecast\nhighs show readings topping 110 degrees as early as Wednesday\nacross southeast California and as early as Thursday across the\nArizona lower deserts. Areas of Major HeatRisk are expected by \nThursday, likely expanding in coverage Friday into the weekend as \nthe heat reaches its peak. Overnight lows will also become an \nissue as surface moisture remains elevated enough to cause lows to\nonly dip into the upper 80s to lower 90s by Thursday. Daytime \nhighs of 113-116 degrees across the western deserts to 110-113 \ndegrees in the Phoenix area are shown for this weekend, while heat\nindices of 115 degrees are likely to appear as early as Thursday \nfor the western deserts. Due to this an Extreme Heat Watch has \nbeen issued for Southeast California Starting Wednesday and then \nexpanding eastwards to include south central Arizona and the \nPhoenix Metro starting Thursday.",
